/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  edbatch01_constant_current  --  a batch electrodialysis rig run at CONSTANT
  CURRENT, and the limiting current falling under it until they cross.

  THE OPERATION.  Five litres of 0.1 mol/kg brine in the diluate tank, five
  litres of 0.5 mol/kg in the concentrate tank, both recirculated through the
  bench stack of `ed03_stack_record` (Eurodia EUR2C-7P18, seven cell pairs,
  0.020 m2 each) at the paper's own 175 L/h.  The rectifier is held at 3.5 A,
  which is 175 A/m2 on one cell pair.

  WHY IT IS A DIFFERENT EXPERIMENT FROM ed01-ed05.  Those are steady: one
  pass, a fixed diluate composition, so the limiting current is evaluated
  ONCE.  Here the diluate tank DEPLETES, and i_lim -- which the
  GeraldesAfonso2010 model predicts from the ion diffusivities and the diluate
  composition -- falls with it, while i does not move at all.  They must
  cross.

  WHAT THE RUN MEASURES (every number below is printed by this run; nothing
  here is a target):

      i                     175.00 A/m2, flat, all 2000 s
      i_lim                 643.04 A/m2 at t = 0
                             88.26 A/m2 at t = 2000 s
      THE CROSSING           t = 1593 s          (i/i_lim reaches 1)
      i/i_lim at the end    1.983
      xi_eff                0.900 -> 0.4539      (the plateau, below)
      demineralisation      86.27 %  ACTUAL
                            91.41 %  what the hand calculation predicts
      the hand calculation's own answer: the tank is EMPTY at t = 2188 s
      concentrate          0.5 -> 0.5863 mol/kg  (x1.173)
      specific energy       1.946 kWh per m3 of diluate treated
      material closure      1.4e-15 on mass, 8.5e-14 on the worst element

  THE LESSON.  Faraday's law at constant current is a STRAIGHT LINE:

      n(t) = n(0) - xi I N t / (z F)

  and it is what a student computes by hand.  The run publishes it as
  `demin_ideal`, evaluated from THIS run's own initial current, own initial
  inventory and own declared xi -- computed, never declared, so the gap
  cannot have been arranged -- beside `demin_actual`.

  The two agree exactly until t = 1593 s and part after it, and the reason is
  printed at the instant it happens: i has caught i_lim.  At the limiting
  current the counter-ion concentration vanishes at the diluate/membrane
  interface and the film can deliver no more of it, so a larger current is
  carried by water-splitting products instead of by salt.  This case DECLARES
  that model -- `overLimiting { model saltFluxPlateau; }`, xi_eff = xi
  i_lim/i, which adds no parameter -- so the depletion stops being linear and
  becomes asymptotic: the straight line hits zero at 2188 s and the tank
  never does.

  WITHOUT the declaration (the default) the current efficiency would stay at
  xi and the two curves would coincide to the integrator's own precision.
  That is not a better answer; it is a model with nothing in it that could
  part from the hand calculation, and the run SAYS so when i crosses i_lim.
  `edbatch02_constant_voltage` is the same rig with the rectifier held at a
  voltage instead: there the current DECAYS, the crossing never happens, and
  the same hand calculation fails for an entirely different reason.

  WHAT IS PINNED AND WHAT IS NOT.  The golden pins the answer THIS model
  gives.  Nothing here is validated against a measured batch electrodialysis:
  the stack record reproduces the paper's bench unit and its limiting-current
  correlation, but no run of that unit in recirculating batch is transcribed
  anywhere in this tree.  `t_overLimiting_s` is resolved to the TIME STEP --
  it is the first accepted step at which i exceeds i_lim, so it moves with
  deltaT (1593 s at deltaT 1, 1592.5 s at deltaT 0.25) while everything else
  is converged to ~1e-8.

  NOT MODELLED, and it matters to how far this run can be trusted: no salt
  diffuses back from the concentrate, no water is transported with the ions,
  the current efficiency is a declared constant apart from the plateau above,
  and the rig is isothermal with the Joule heat removed by no modelled duty
  (which is why the campaign energy balance reports UNAVAILABLE and says
  why).  The full list is in the unit's own header.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        rig;
        type        batchElectrodialysis;

        operation
        {
            stack          EUR2C-7P18;   // the HARDWARE, as a record (ed03)

            //  The pump setting.  A steady stack reads its crossflow off an
            //  inlet stream; a batch rig has no stream, so the recirculation
            //  rate is declared here and nowhere else.  175 L/h is the
            //  paper's own middle flow: u = 0.0791 m/s, Re 68.5, inside the
            //  50-86 band its correlation was fitted over.
            diluateFlow    175 L/h;

            //  CONSTANT CURRENT.  175 A/m2 on one cell pair -- below the
            //  record's estimated 200 A/m2 ceiling, and below i_lim at t = 0.
            current        3.5;           // A (raw SI)

            xi             0.9;           // current efficiency (declared)
            E_electrodes   1.5;           // V (raw SI), lumped, announced

            //  THE OVER-LIMITING REGIME, declared.  Without this block the
            //  current efficiency stays at xi past the crossing and the run
            //  announces that it is then extrapolating a model outside its
            //  validity.  With it, the counter-ion transfer is frozen at its
            //  limiting value -- no new parameter.
            overLimiting   { model saltFluxPlateau; }
        }
    }
);
